This project (EE-456: RF & mm-Wave Active Circuits) designs a **15 GHz low-noise amplifier (LNA) matching network** for the **MGF4941AL InGaAs HEMT** at **VDS = 2 V** and **IDS = 10 mA**.  

Unlike Project 1 (pure maximum gain), the objective here is a **practical compromise** that satisfies **gain, noise, and matching** requirements simultaneously, with results validated independently in **MATLAB** and **Keysight ADS**.

**Course:** EE-456 Microwave Active Circuits  
**Assignment:** Design Project 02  
**Toolchain:** MATLAB + Keysight ADS (ideal transmission-line matching + Touchstone verification)  
**Center frequency:** \( f_0 = 15~\text{GHz} \) (sweep: ~14–16 GHz)

---

## Design Specs (Course Targets @ \( f_0 \))

- **Transducer gain:** \( G_T \ge 12~\text{dB} \)
- **Noise figure:** \( NF \le 0.6~\text{dB} \)
- **Matching constraint:** \( \text{VSWR} \le 1.5 \) (input and output)

---

## Strategy: MAG vs \( F_{\min} \) vs “Meets Everything” (Design 2)

The design begins with device S-parameters and noise parameters at **15 GHz**, then uses Smith-chart geometry to select source and load terminations that satisfy all constraints.

- **MAG design:** maximizes gain, but noise figure is too high (fails NF spec).
- **\( F_{\min} \) design:** minimizes noise figure, but gain is insufficient (fails gain spec).
- **Design 2 (final):** selects \( \Gamma_S \) and \( \Gamma_L \) from the feasible region defined by  
  **noise-figure circles**, **available-gain circles**, and **stability constraints**, then realizes them using **single-stub transmission-line networks**.

